<h2 id="nj-taxes">New Jersey taxes and Newark rules</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>State income tax:</strong> New Jersey uses graduated rates from 1.4% to 10.75%. Most side hustlers fall in the lower to middle brackets.</li>
  <li><strong>No resident city income tax:</strong> Newark doesn't tax residents' income directly (its payroll tax falls on employers, not on you as a sole proprietor).</li>
  <li><strong>Self-employment tax:</strong> 15.3% federal on net profit. Estimate it with the <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales tax:</strong> 6.625% statewide on most goods. Parts of Newark are in an Urban Enterprise Zone where qualifying registered businesses can charge a reduced rate; check eligibility with the state.</li>
  <li><strong>Registration:</strong> there's no general business license, but most businesses register with the NJ Division of Revenue. Home improvement work, food sales and short-term rentals have their own requirements; Newark restricts short-term rentals and requires a city permit, so confirm the current rules before listing.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout-warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> If you live in Newark but drive or deliver into New York, you may owe New York nonresident tax on income earned there. New Jersey gives a credit for taxes paid to New York, but you'll need to file both returns.</div>

